  The <hlink|exact statement|statement.tm> distinguishes the public theorem
  from additional properties of its construction. The <hlink|method
  page|method.tm> describes the checks and their limits.

  <section|Does this prove a mass gap?><label|does-this-prove-a-mass-gap>

  No mass gap is proved. The public theorem gives a spectrum condition and a
  unique invariant vacuum in a selected phase, but neither supplies a
  positive lower bound on nonzero excitation energies. The detailed
  construction's low-energy and heat bounds are polynomial; they do not
  establish either a gap or gaplessness. See <hlink|what is not
  claimed|statement.tm#4-what-is-not-claimed> and <hlink|the reconstruction
  account|summary.tm#part-v-phases-and-relativistic-reconstruction>.

  <section|Is this a solution of the Clay Millennium
  problem?><label|is-this-a-solution-of-the-clay-millennium-problem>

  It does not establish the full Yang\UMills existence and mass-gap claim
  discussed in the Jaffe\UWitten formulation: the mass gap is absent from its
  conclusions. It constructs theories with the properties specified on the
  <hlink|statement page|statement.tm#public-theorem>, subject to the
  qualifications there. The <hlink|comparison with
  Jaffe\UWitten|statement.tm#53-the-jaffewitten-formulation-jw> explains the
  differences; this repository does not certify a prize claim.

  <section|What makes the constructed theory
  Yang\UMills?><label|what-makes-the-constructed-theory-yangmills>

  Its mixed Euclidean correlations come from Wilson lattice gauge theories
  for the specified compact matrix group, with local gauge-invariant
  insertions and logarithmically growing bare inverse coupling. The
  construction identifies local fields with quotients of curvature-polynomial
  labels, constructs a normalized stress tensor and proves short-distance
  comparison with free Maxwell fields. Some of these identifications belong
  to the construction rather than the public theorem itself. See <hlink|the
  objects and their interpretation|statement.tm#1-the-objects> and <hlink|the
  additional conclusions|statement.tm#detailed-theorem>.

  <section|What is new here?><label|what-is-new-here>

  The mathematical result presented here is the construction with the precise
  limits, reconstruction, OPE and trace properties in the
  <hlink|statement|statement.tm>. The <hlink|summary's discussion of earlier
  work|summary.tm#relation-to-earlier-work-and-where-the-difficulty-lies>
  describes features of this argument, including the common family of
  observables, fixed-volume convergence and recovery of continuous symmetry.
  These descriptions are not a systematic literature comparison or a claim of
  priority for each ingredient. This repository's contribution is an
  explanatory map of the released formal proof; the <hlink|full
  manuscript|manuscript/index.tm> is under construction. For a
  component-by-component account of which parts of the proof follow Balaban's
  programme, which follow OpenAI's 2026 papers on two-dimensional sigma
  models, which are standard and which appear new, see <hlink|Where the ideas
  come from|provenance.tm>.

  <section|Why is there only a subsequence of
  volumes?><label|why-is-there-only-a-subsequence-of-volumes>

  The infinite-volume argument uses compactness to extract a convergent
  subsequence from any prescribed increasing sequence of volume indices. It
  does not show that different extractions give the same limit. By contrast,
  at each admissible fixed volume the ultraviolet limit uses the whole
  construction-chosen sequence of spacings. See <hlink|ordered
  convergence|statement.tm#ordered-convergence>,
  <hlink|IV.1|outline/units/IV-1.tm> and <hlink|the
  summary|summary.tm#part-iv-infinite-volume>.

  <section|Why are there phases, and how is the selected phase
  chosen?><label|why-are-there-phases-and-how-is-the-selected-phase-chosen>

  The lattice limit is a mixed correlation hierarchy, represented as an
  average over a compact space of phases with a probability measure of full
  support. In the construction the selected phase is chosen only to have a
  one-dimensional ground-state space; no trace condition is imposed in that
  choice. The public theorem reconstructs that selected phase, while the
  detailed theorem reconstructs every phase. Neither equality of the phases
  nor uniqueness of the infinite-volume construction follows from the unique
  vacuum in each reconstructed theory. See <hlink|phase
  selection|statement.tm#continuum-hierarchy>,
  <hlink|V.1|outline/units/V-1.tm> and <hlink|V.3|outline/units/V-3.tm>.

  <section|What exactly does \Pnot free\Q
  mean?><label|what-exactly-does-not-free-mean>

  The selected two-point function of a centered degree-4 field on the time
  axis has a positive <math|r<rsup|-8>*log<rsup|-2><around|(|1/r|)>> leading
  law. No finite sum of the products of mass-shell kernels specified in
  <hlink|Definition 1.15|statement.tm#wick-profiles> agrees with that
  function throughout any interval next to zero. This does not exclude
  continuous-mass generalized free fields, infinite sums or every composite
  of Gaussian fields. The detailed construction supplies additional Wick and
  Maxwell tests with their own hypotheses; see <hlink|the trace
  test|statement.tm#trace-test> and <hlink|D9|statement.tm#detailed-theorem>.

  <section|Why correlations rather than a measure on
  connections?><label|why-correlations-rather-than-a-measure-on-connections>

  The constructed continuum objects are correlation functions away from
  coincident points, their phase decomposition and reconstructed relativistic
  fields. The detailed theorem also supplies distributional extensions across
  coincident points, without asserting uniqueness of those extensions. It
  does not construct a continuum probability measure on connections; this is
  a limit of what was proved, not a claim that such a measure cannot exist.
  See <hlink|the nonclaims|statement.tm#4-what-is-not-claimed> and <hlink|OPE
  and contact extensions|summary.tm#operator-product-expansion-and-contact-extensions>.

  <section|Does the OPE agree with perturbation theory to all
  orders?><label|does-the-ope-agree-with-perturbation-theory-to-all-orders>

  No such agreement is proved. For every finite truncation the theorem gives
  coefficient and remainder bounds on separated configurations, with one
  coefficient system for the mixed state and every phase and for all
  spectators. The small-radius threshold can depend on the spectator labels
  and separation parameters. Coefficient uniqueness and convergence of the
  infinite expansion are not asserted; see <hlink|the exact
  OPE|statement.tm#operator-product-expansion> and
  <hlink|VII.4|outline/units/VII-4.tm>.

  <section|What was checked by Lean, and what by people or
  AI?><label|what-was-checked-by-lean-and-what-by-people-or-ai>

  Lean's kernel checks formal proof terms against formal types; the released
  verification log records type, axiom and kernel-replay checks for
  <hlink|seventeen named results|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/verification/joint-headlines.txt>.
  The statement page, summaries, glossary and outlines are AI-assisted
  interpretations and explanations, with the review scopes described in
  <hlink|method.md|method.tm#statement-and-explanation-checks>. The
  Yang\UMills group's <hlink|assessment|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/YM/blob/55ba9fc36293b3f4d52c6b1f813169a0be76ee98/docs/assessment-1.pdf>
  is a separate account of its validation. Collaboration on this repository
  does not mean that every outline has received an independent human proof
  review.

  <section|Is the English statement faithful to the
  Lean?><label|is-the-english-statement-faithful-to-the-lean>

  The public theorem on <hlink|statement.md|statement.tm> was transcribed and
  compared definition by definition with the pinned Lean, including
  quantifiers, field spaces and the order of choices. This is a documented
  interpretation check, and the page exposes both the formal text and its
  <hlink|source correspondence|statement.tm#correspondence-with-the-lean-source>
  for inspection. The additional construction clauses D1\UD16 have a narrower
  review scope: D3 and D5\UD15 were not all unfolded to the last definition.
  A Lean check of the proof does not itself verify any English transcription.

  <section|How much should I trust an edited
  outline?><label|how-much-should-i-trust-an-edited-outline>

  \PEdited\Q records a third round of AI review of the exposition after
  drafting and source checking. It does not mean every subsidiary analytic
  estimate was independently read or that the outline is a complete informal
  proof. Each unit's \PWhat remains unexplained\Q section states local
  limits, and a draft outline does not imply its formal theorem is unproved.
  See <hlink|status meanings|method.tm#unit-outline-status>, the <hlink|unit
  index|outline/units.tm> and the <hlink|summary's incomplete
  steps|summary.tm#steps-whose-informal-account-is-incomplete>.

  <section|Does the dependency graph verify the
  explanations?><label|does-the-dependency-graph-verify-the-explanations>

  No: it records declaration references in elaborated types and proof terms.
  Agreement with OpenAI's record tests the reachable sets for the seventeen
  named results, not every edge, the mathematical role of each reference or
  an English proof sketch. Cohen's \<kappa\> = 0.72 concerns agreement
  between readers' importance labels; it is not a correctness score. See
  <hlink|graph scope|method.tm#1-the-dependency-graph> and <hlink|statement
  selection|method.tm#3-key-statements>.

  <section|How can I check the result myself?><label|how-can-i-check-the-result-myself>

  For a mathematical clause, the <hlink|clause-to-explanation
  guide|statement.tm#clause-to-explanation-guide> connects its exact
  definition to outline results, the summary and pinned Lean references. For
  formal verification, <hlink|method.md|method.tm#source-compilation-and-kernel-checking>
  gives a fresh-checkout build and the released type, axiom and replay
  checks. The <hlink|compiled release|index.tm#compiled-lean-files> helps
  load and explore the source, but reusing those files is not an independent
  compilation. Reading an outline and rebuilding a formal proof answer
  different questions, as the table below explains.

  <section|How do I report a mismatch or cite what I
  read?><label|how-do-i-report-a-mismatch-or-cite-what-i-read>

  <hlink|Open an issue|https://github.com/scottnarmstrong/yang-mills-informalization/issues>
  with the page and heading, the sentence in question, and a proposed
  correction or pinned Lean link. Include the repository revision and unit or
  result identifier so that the wording can be recovered after later edits.
  Use <hlink|CITATION.cff|https://github.com/scottnarmstrong/yang-mills-informalization/blob/main/CITATION.cff>
  and the <hlink|citation guidance|index.tm#how-to-cite>; <hlink|change
  notes|CHANGELOG.tm> record reader-facing corrections.

  <section|What each check establishes><label|what-each-check-establishes>

